+{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI1ODUxMjQ2","is_bot":false,"login":"dbeallor","name":""},"body":"Replace the \"Best Albums\" spreadsheet with an app where I search a catalog of albums, rank my favorites per year with cover art, and get artist and decade lists from a scoring system I can change. This ticket is the requirements spec for the MVP. It is a spec, not code. The follow-up development tickets are blocked on it.\n\n## Today\nThe repo has only a README, so there is no code. The working system is the spreadsheet `Best Albums.xlsx` (kept by the user, not in the repo). What it does, read from the export:\n\n- **Albums sheet.** One row per album, ranked or honorable mention. Columns: rank, album, artist, year, decade, honorable mention flag, decade top 25 candidate flag, decade top 25 rank, a \"new\" flag, points, total albums in year, year weight, weighted score. About 674 rows (294 ranked, 380 honorable mentions) across 1955 to 2024 and about 393 artists. Years run from 1 album (1983) to 41 (2019, 2021). Some years have fewer than 10 ranked albums.\n- **Scoring System sheet.** Rank 1 to 10 earns 10 down to 1 point. An honorable mention earns 0.25.\n- **Year weight.** Each year gets a weight from the number of albums listed that year (ranked plus honorable mentions). Fitting the sheet's values gives `weight = (N / maxN) ^ 0.25`, where `maxN` is the largest yearly count (41). It matches the sheet for N = 1, 2, 3, 20, 32 and 41. The formula itself is not in the export, so this is a fit, not a copy.\n- **Weighted score.** `points x year weight` per album.\n- **Ranked sheet.** A per-year grid: ranks 1 to 10 plus an unordered honorable mention list.\n- **Decade top 25.** Albums flagged as candidates and given a rank 1 to 25 inside their decade. Filled for the 1960s, 2000s and 2010s.\n- **Artist Stats, By Decade, Weighted Score and Artist Summary sheets.** Artist rankings, grouped as 60s and 70s, 80s, 90s and 2000s, and 2010s and 2020s, plus an overall rollup. Their formulas did not survive the export (the cells are empty), so the rollup rule is assumed to be the sum of weighted scores per artist.\n\nThe user says they have always been unhappy with the scoring system. They have not said what is wrong with it.\n\n## Scope\nWrite the spec as `docs/superpowers/specs/<date>-album-ranker-mvp.md`. It should settle:\n\n- **Users and shape.** Who uses it (one person or many), web or native, accounts or none, where data lives. Pick the simplest option that works and say why.\n- **MVP features.** At least: catalog search with cover art, a per-year ranking screen (drag and drop, covers, honorable mentions), a configurable scoring system, artist views by decade and overall, decade top 25 lists, and import of the existing spreadsheet.\n- **Scoring.** Keep the current rule as the default and as a regression baseline. Say which parts are configurable in the MVP (points per rank, honorable mention value, year weight curve, rollup rule). Record the open question of what the user dislikes about it, and ask them.\n- **Decade top 25.** Decide whether it is a hand-ranked list (as in the sheet) or derived from weighted score.\n- **Albums versus releases.** How an album in the catalog maps to one ranked entry (reissues, editions, compilations), and what happens when an album is missing from the catalog.\n- **Data model outline.** Enough that the data model ticket can start.\n- **Non-goals** and a **Calls I made** list for anything the user did not specify.\n- **Acceptance for the whole MVP.** For example: importing the spreadsheet and applying the default scoring reproduces the spreadsheet's weighted scores.\n\n## Scoring: open question\n\nThe user said the weights feel arbitrary but could not say more, and asked for a critique and a better option, possibly one with no scores at all. This is the Chief of Staff's read of the spreadsheet (754 rows, years 1955 to 2024; the Today section above counts about 674 albums, a gap not yet explained). The user approves the final choice, and it is a requirement of this spec.\n\n**How it works today.** Rank 1 to 10 earns 10 to 1 points, an honorable mention earns 0.25. That is multiplied by a year weight of `(albums listed that year / max albums listed in any year) ^ 0.25`, so the busiest years (2019 and 2021, 41 albums) weigh 1.0 and one-album years weigh 0.395. Artist and decade lists sum the weighted scores.\n\n**Problems.**\n\n1. The year weight counts how many albums the user logged that year. That tracks how much they listened or how recent the year is, not how strong the year was.\n2. The 0.25 exponent is a tuning knob with no justification.\n3. A #1 album in a one-album year is worth 3.95 against 10 in a big year, though it was equally the favourite of its year.\n4. Ranks are ordinal within a year, so nothing in the data can calibrate one year against another.\n5. Linear points make #1 vs #2 count the same as #9 vs #10.\n6. Artist totals are sums, so they reward catalogue size and volume over quality. For example The Beatles have 13 albums and 81.3 weighted points.\n\n**Options to evaluate in this spec.**\n\n- (a) Drop the year weight, keep rank points, and add diminishing returns to artist sums.\n- (b) Add a cross-year signal, such as a tier or star rating per album, so years can be compared honestly.\n- (c) Pairwise comparison with Elo or Bradley-Terry, using head-to-head matchups the UI can serve. This gives a global ranking with no hand-set weights.\n- (d) Rank-based ordering with no scores at all: count of #1s, top 3s and top 10s, compared in that order.\n- (e) A Bayesian or average-based artist score that separates quality from volume.\n\n**Chief of Staff leaning.** (b) or (c) for albums, plus (e) for artists, with (a) kept as the compatibility baseline so the spreadsheet import reproduces today's numbers. The scoring engine (#7) must support swappable algorithms so this choice does not block it.\n\n**Needed from this spec.** Compare the options, pick one for the MVP, and put the choice to the user for approval.\n\n## Acceptance\n- The spec is committed and approved by the user.\n- Every item in Scope has an answer or an explicit open question.\n- The follow-up tickets (scaffold, data model, catalog search, ranking UI, scoring engine, artist views, decade lists, import) still match the spec. Any ticket that no longer fits is edited or canceled.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Writing application code.\n- Picking a visual design beyond what the ranking screen needs to be specified.","createdAt":"2026-11-20T23:08:48Z","labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOV2EaVs8AAAADEJXHTQ","name":"type:feature","description":"Type: Feature","color":"1d76db"},{"id":"LA_kwDOV2EaVs8AAAADEJXIFQ","name":"agent","description":"Worked by the agent team","color":"0052cc"}],"state":"CLOSED","title":"Write the MVP requirements spec for the album ranking app"}
